AB  - The use of entomopathogenic fungi as a microbial control has advantages over chemical insecticides, because they exhibits natural mortality and have less environmental impact. Production of biological control agents such as Beauveria bassiana are limited to define the optimal concentration of conidia and potential of native strains for use in the grasshopper is unknown. In the study, the mortality of Sphenarium purpurascens was evaluated in vitro, by bio-preparations at different concentrations of conidia of the MA-Bb1 strain and two preservatives (diatomite earth (D) and zeolite). The different bio-preparations obtained concentrations of 2.78 108 to 10.30 108 conidia/ml, showing a linear tendency with respect to the mortality of nymphs of the 2nd and 4th stage, as well as adults of S. purpurascens in vitro, which was directly proportional to the concentration of conidia of B. bassiana. The greatest concentration of conidia was in the bio-preparation (Z + D + Bb1 [1/1]) with 10.30 108 conidia/ml, a viability of 93% and mortality greater than 90% for the different stages, as well as for the adult of S. purpurascens. Results showed the feasibility of the use of the native strain MA-Bb1 of B. bassiana in combination with the diatomite and zeolite earth for biological control of the grasshopper in laboratory conditions.
